\import Arith.Nat
\import Arith.Int (iabs)

-- λ□'s primitive integer: `Uint63`, and the normal form that defines it.
--
-- WHAT IT IS. `LambdaBox.ard`'s `prim (primInt63, i)` carries an unbounded Arend
-- `Int`, but the language it models does not: a λ□ primitive int is a 63-bit
-- UNSIGNED machine integer with cyclic arithmetic, i.e. a residue mod 2^63.
--   * MetaRocq erasure/theories/EPrimitive.v -- `primIntModel (i :
--     PrimInt63.int)`, and `PrimInt63.int` is Rocq's `Uint63`.
--   * lean-to-lambdabox -- `PrimModel .primInt := BitVec 63`.
--   * Rocq refman, "Primitive objects / Primitive integers" -- 63-bit machine
--     integer, unsigned view `Uint63`, signed view `Sint63`.
--
-- WHY IT LIVES HERE, outside both `Formal/` and `JavaAxioms.ard`. Three places
-- need it and they are on opposite sides of the translation:
--   * `JavaAxioms.ard` -- to emit a normalized literal (the compiler);
--   * `Formal/LbAxioms.ard` -- λ□'s own oracle for `prim_*_int` (the source
--     semantics);
--   * `Formal/RtInt63.ard` -- the model of the `Rt.PRIM_*_INT63` family (the
--     target semantics).
-- Sharing one definition is not convenience here, it IS the claim: the point of
-- the int63 representation is that the backend computes what the source
-- language says, and three copies of "mod 2^63" that happened to agree would
-- state that much more weakly. Contrast `Formal/RtLong.ard`'s `wrap64`, which
-- is deliberately NOT shared -- it is the JVM's rule (JLS 4.2.1/4.2.2), a
-- different specification that merely has the same shape.

-- Named for its ROLE. `Formal/RtLong.ard` uses `pow2_63` for the point at
-- which a JVM `long` turns negative -- the same number, a different meaning --
-- and a module importing both would otherwise get a clash warning.
\func int63Mod : Nat => 9223372036854775808

-- And the other kind's modulus. `primInt64`'s payload is the 64-bit BIT
-- PATTERN, unsigned in [0, 2^64); the signed reading a JVM `long` gives it is
-- applied when the value is emitted or compared, exactly as
-- `Formal/RtLong.ard`'s `wrap64` does. Kept here beside `int63Mod` because
-- `LambdaBox.ard` needs both to state `PrimModel`, and the AST must not import
-- a `Formal/` module.
\func int64Mod : Nat => 18446744073709551616

-- The signed reading of a 64-bit bit pattern: the top half of [0, 2^64) is the
-- negatives, which is what a JVM `long` means by the same bits.
\func signed64 (i : Fin int64Mod) : Int =>
  \case (i : Nat) Nat.- int63Mod \with {
    | pos _ => (i : Nat) Nat.- int64Mod   -- i >= 2^63, equality included
    | neg (suc _) => pos i
  }

\func wrap64u (x : Int) : Fin int64Mod \elim x
  | pos n => finMod n
  | neg n => finMod (iabs (int64Mod Nat.- (n Nat.mod int64Mod)))

-- `x` reduced into [0, 2^63): the `Uint63` normal form.
--
-- IT RETURNS A `Fin`, which is the point. `PrimModel primInt63` is
-- `Fin int63Mod`, so the ONLY way to build a λ□ primitive-int payload is
-- through a function that normalizes -- an out-of-range primitive is not
-- representable rather than merely discouraged, and no caller can forget.
-- `finMod` (arend-lib `Arith.Nat`) needs no positivity proof because the
-- modulus is a literal, i.e. syntactically `suc _`.
--
-- UNSIGNED -- there is no reinterpretation of the upper half of the range as
-- negative, because `Uint63` has no sign. That single difference from `wrap64`
-- is the int63/int64 distinction.
--
-- The `neg` clause is `2^63 - (n mod 2^63)`, taken mod 2^63 again so that
-- `neg 0` lands on 0 rather than on 2^63 -- `neg 0` is DEFINITIONALLY `pos 0`
-- (Prelude's constructor condition on `Int`), and the two clauses must agree
-- there.
\func wrap63 (x : Int) : Fin int63Mod \elim x
  | pos n => finMod n
  | neg n => finMod (iabs (int63Mod Nat.- (n Nat.mod int63Mod)))

-- The same as an `Int`, which is the form the Java side and the printers want.
-- Always non-negative.
\func norm63 (x : Int) : Int => pos (wrap63 x)
